CODECS(1)                   User Commands                  CODECS(1)

NAME
    codecs - list, identify and convert image and audio formats

SYNOPSIS
    codecs
    codecs info file...
    codecs convert [-f name] [-b bits] [-s size] [-o options] in out

DESCRIPTION
    The formats of minios are modules in /lib/codecs, which the codec
    library loads at run time. Every program that opens images or
    audio reads the formats of all modules. The environment variable
    CODEC_PATH gives another module directory.

    Without arguments codecs lists every codec with its capabilities
    (D decodes, E encodes, S renders at any size, A has animations),
    its kind, its module, its description, its MIME types and its
    extensions.

    info gives the format of each file, found by its content and then
    by its extension, and describes it: the size of an image and
    whether it has alpha, for an animation its frames, the length of one
    play and the number of plays, or the rate, channels, sample size,
    frames and duration of audio.

    convert decodes in and encodes it in the format of the extension of
    out, or with the codec name. Both formats must be of one kind. An
    animation remains an animation when the target format has
    animations, and becomes its first frame otherwise.

OPTIONS
    -f name      encode with this codec, whatever the extension
    -b bits      sample size of the converted audio (the source's)
    -s size      size in pixels at which a vector image is rendered
    -o options   options of the audio encoder as name=value pairs
                 separated by commas. The Vorbis encoder takes
                 quality (-0.1 to 1.0, 0.4 by default) and floor
                 (1 by default, or 0). The MP3 encoder takes
                 bitrate in kbit/s (32 to 320 at 32, 44.1 and 48 kHz,
                 8 to 160 at 16, 22.05 and 24 kHz, 8 to 64 at 8,
                 11.025 and 12 kHz; 128, 64 and 32 for two channels
                 by default, half of that for one)

EXIT STATUS
    0 on success, 1 when a file cannot be read, identified, decoded,
    encoded or written, and 2 for wrong usage.

EXAMPLES
    codecs
    codecs info /usr/share/sounds/chime.wav
    codecs convert shot.png shot.bmp
    codecs convert -s 64 /usr/share/icons/app-default.svg icon.png
    codecs convert -b 24 chime.wav chime24.wav
    codecs convert -o quality=0.7 chime.wav chime.ogg

SEE ALSO
